  "summary": "Recent preprints target scaling limits in sensing: super-Heisenberg time-dependent estimation, robust hypergraph probes, k-local thermometry, sqrt(N) spectroscopy.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is identifying which physical resources set achievable precision in parameter estimation, and how to access them under noise. On the control side, subspace-twirled dynamical decoupling is proposed to reach super-Heisenberg scaling for time-dependent signals without adaptive control. Task-specific algorithms for discrete-frequency spectroscopy give a sqrt(N) speedup over frequency scanning for detection. For probe states, complete weighted hypergraph states yield analytic QFI with Heisenberg scaling under collective-spin sensing, robust to erasure. In equilibrium thermometry, k-local Hamiltonians outperform 2-local models and approach the heat-capacity optimum as locality grows. Inference is addressed via quantum extreme learning, which avoids explicit likelihood construction. Adjacent work treats multiparameter metrology alongside quantum-battery performance in a hydrogen-like hyperfine spin system. A further preprint develops information thermodynamics for closed many-body dynamics. Another reports enhanced post-measurement entanglement in an Ising register on a quantum computer.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
